From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: recover the whole VE on a watchdog timeout

A `frame processing timed out!` currently ends video on this board for the
rest of the boot. Every client is affected, not just the one that submitted
the bad job: cedrus is a single m2m device, so the next GStreamer pipeline
or libva client inherits a dead engine and times out in turn. The only
recovery found in practice was a reboot, and this became a documented trap
for the test harness ("reboot between runs once anything has timed out")
rather than the defect it is.

Two things are wrong in cedrus_watchdog(), both on the recovery path rather
than in the decode itself.

FIRST, IT RESETS HALF THE ENGINE. reset_control_reset(dev->rstc) pulses the
"ve" line only. That is all the SoCs upstream supports have, but this board's
video-codec node carries reset-names = "ve", "ve3", and every other power
transition in this driver already handles both -- cedrus_hw_resume()
deasserts ve then ve3, cedrus_hw_suspend() asserts ve3 then ve, and
cedrus_stop_streaming() has pulsed both on every teardown since patch 0040.
The timeout path was simply never updated when the second reset arrived for
this SoC, so a stalled VE3 half stays stalled.

SECOND, IT ACKNOWLEDGES NOTHING. The watchdog finishes the job without
calling the codec's irq_disable()/irq_clear(), unlike cedrus_irq() which
does both. The VE interrupt is IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH here, and once the
watchdog has run, cedrus_irq() takes its `!cancel_delayed_work()` early
return and acknowledges nothing -- so a status bit the engine left set is
re-raised indefinitely.

Fix both: quiesce the interrupt, then pulse both reset lines in the order the
teardown path uses.

Behind sunxi_cedrus.watchdog_full_reset, default on and writable at runtime,
so both behaviours can be compared inside ONE boot with the same CMA layout
and the same workload. That is the design that made patch 0040's result
trustworthy, and it is what lets the broken arm be demonstrated on demand
rather than argued from a log.

Safety of the sequence is not assumed: the device is powered throughout,
because pm_runtime_resume_and_get() is taken in start_streaming() and held
until stop_streaming(), so register access and reset pulses from the
watchdog work are legal. And the same two-reset pulse already runs once per
decode in stop_streaming -- several hundred times in the first ten minutes
of a decode soak -- without incident.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
@@ -128,6 +129,21 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Recover the whole engine when the watchdog fires, rather than half of it
+ * (sunxi_cedrus.watchdog_full_reset, default on; writable at runtime).
+ *
+ * The knob exists so both behaviours can be compared inside ONE boot, with the
+ * same CMA layout, the same clocks and the same workload -- the A/B that patch
+ * 0040 used for stop_reset, and the reason its result was trustworthy where an
+ * across-flash comparison would not have been. Turn it off to reproduce the
+ * upstream wedge on demand.
+ */
+static bool watchdog_full_reset = true;
+module_param(watchdog_full_reset, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(watchdog_full_reset,
+		 "on a decode timeout, quiesce the IRQ and reset both VE reset lines");
+
 static irqreturn_t cedrus_irq(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct cedrus_dev *dev = data;
@@ -180,7 +196,36 @@
 		return;
 
 	v4l2_err(&dev->v4l2_dev, "frame processing timed out!\n");
+
+	if (watchdog_full_reset) {
+		/*
+		 * Quiesce the interrupt before resetting anything. The engine
+		 * may still be asserting it, and this line is
+		 * IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH: once the watchdog has run, cedrus_irq()
+		 * takes its `!cancel_delayed_work()` early return and
+		 * acknowledges nothing, so a status bit left set is re-raised
+		 * indefinitely.
+		 */
+		ctx->current_codec->irq_disable(ctx);
+		ctx->current_codec->irq_clear(ctx);
+
+		/*
+		 * Reset the WHOLE engine. Upstream pulses only "ve" because
+		 * that is all the SoCs it supports have; the H713's VE3 carries
+		 * a second reset line, and every other power transition here
+		 * handles both -- cedrus_hw_resume() deasserts ve then ve3,
+		 * cedrus_hw_suspend() asserts ve3 then ve, and
+		 * cedrus_stop_streaming() has pulsed both on every teardown
+		 * since patch 0040. Order matches that teardown path, which
+		 * runs once per decode and is therefore already exercised
+		 * hundreds of times in a single soak.
+		 */
+		if (dev->rstc_ve3)
+			reset_control_reset(dev->rstc_ve3);
+	}
+
 	reset_control_reset(dev->rstc);
+
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_done_and_job_finish(ctx->dev->m2m_dev, ctx->fh.m2m_ctx,
 					 VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
 }
